{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "vaipakam-offchain-data-warm",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-05-23",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
  },
  // Triggers: ONE cron per day at 03:17 UTC (off-peak across UTC /
  // IST / PT). The minute is non-zero on purpose so the run doesn't
  // collide with every other team-on-the-internet's :00 crons
  // fighting for B2's incoming HTTP capacity at exact-minute marks.
  //
  // The single cron does TWO things on Monday and ONE thing on every
  // other day:
  //   - Daily (Mon-Sun): nightly backup runs, encrypts, uploads to B2.
  //   - Monday only: ALSO runs the weekly healthcheck IN PARALLEL with
  //     the nightly backup (two independent ctx.waitUntil() calls, no
  //     shared state, different scoped B2 keys, different B2 endpoints).
  //
  // Why one cron instead of two: Cloudflare Workers free plan caps an
  // account at 5 cron triggers total. apps/{keeper,agent,indexer} take
  // one each (every-minute), and this Worker takes the fourth — 4 of 5
  // in use today. The ops/lz-watcher slot was freed when that Worker was
  // deleted (#1440) and is currently SPARE: ops/mesh-watcher is the
  // intended occupant but is code-complete and UNDEPLOYED, so it holds no
  // slot yet. Deploying it takes the account to 5 of 5. Splitting backup +
  // healthcheck into two crons here would therefore push past the cap once
  // mesh-watcher lands, and the deploy fails with CF API error 10072.
  // Folding healthcheck under the same cron keeps the budget intact. If/when the account upgrades to Workers Paid
  // ($5/mo, removes the cap), this can split back into two crons.
  "triggers": {
    "crons": ["17 3 * * *"],
  },
  // D1 — read-only bindings to the two production databases this
  // Worker backs up. The shared vaipakam-warm holds the indexer
  // tables + the diag / legal-hold registers. (vaipakam-lz-alerts-db
  // was the lz-watcher's separate alerts DB, kept distinct from
  // vaipakam-warm for the trust-boundary reasons documented in
  // CLAUDE.md's D1-schema-discipline section).
  //
  // The Worker NEVER writes to either DB; the bindings are present
  // only so the cron can SELECT rows for export. We deliberately
  // don't add a third D1 for backup bookkeeping (last-run-id,
  // success/failure log) — that lives in `wrangler tail` + the
  // healthcheck alert path, which is enough at Stage A scale.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB_ARCHIVE",
      "database_name": "vaipakam-archive",
      "database_id": "3cffebf5-b652-4da7-953c-9e1d143ad2fe",
      "remote": false,
    },
    // #1440 — the DB_LZ_ALERTS binding to `vaipakam-lz-alerts-db` was
    // removed here. The `vaipakam-lz-watcher` Worker was deleted on
    // 2026-07-28 (post-T-068 it polled a decommissioned LayerZero stack,
    // including the VpfiBuyAdapter surface the #687-A excision removed), so
    // its database holds only alert-dedup state and block cursors for a
    // dead transport. This binding was its last consumer.
    //
    // ORDER MATTERS: this removal ships and runs one clean nightly BEFORE
    // the D1 itself is deleted — dropping the database first would have
    // broken the backup, and quietly, at 03:17.
  ],
  // R2 — read-only binding to the legal-vault. The Worker streams
  // every object out and re-uploads to B2; no writes back to R2.
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "R2_LEGAL_VAULT",
      "bucket_name": "vaipakam-legal-vault",
    },
  ],
  // Public vars. Real secrets live in `wrangler secret put`. Two
  // B2 key pairs (write-scoped + read-scoped) — see src/env.ts for
  // the trust-boundary rationale.
  //   BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY        — 32-byte hex AES-256-GCM key,
  //                                  kept OFFLINE outside Cloudflare
  //                                  (1Password / pass). Never rotated
  //                                  automatically; manual rotation
  //                                  procedure in docs/ops/OffChainRestore.md.
  //   B2_WRITE_ACCESS_KEY_ID       — Application Key id, scoped
  //                                  write-only to the backup bucket
  //                                  (listBuckets + writeFiles — NOT
  //                                  listFiles; #1450 r27). Used by
  //                                  the nightly cron only. A CF
  //                                  compromise that steals THIS key
  //                                  alone can corrupt FUTURE archives
  //                                  and cannot delete anything.
  //                                  It canNOT be assumed to be alone,
  //                                  though: this Worker also binds the
  //                                  READ key below, which carries
  //                                  listFiles — so one compromised
  //                                  environment can enumerate an
  //                                  existing nonce and overwrite AT
  //                                  that key. The immutable-naming
  //                                  guard rests on withholding
  //                                  listFiles here, and binding both
  //                                  keys together defeats it.
  //   B2_WRITE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY   — matching secret of above.
  //   B2_READ_ACCESS_KEY_ID        — Application Key id, scoped
  //                                  read-only to the backup bucket
  //                                  (listBuckets + listFiles +
  //                                  readFiles). Used by the weekly
  //                                  healthcheck only. A CF compromise
  //                                  yields AES-256-GCM ciphertext;
  //                                  the offline encryption key
  //                                  protects the plaintext.
  //   B2_READ_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY    — matching secret of above.
  //   TG_OPS_BOT_TOKEN             — Telegram bot dedicated to
  //                                  internal-ops alerts (this
  //                                  Worker; ops/lz-watcher retired).
  //                                  DELIBERATELY DISTINCT from
  //                                  `TG_BOT_TOKEN`, which is the
  //                                  user-facing bot used by
  //                                  apps/keeper + apps/agent.
  //                                  Splitting the bots bounds the
  //                                  blast radius of a token leak:
  //                                  a user-bot compromise can't
  //                                  spoof ops alerts (where the
  //                                  operator acts on detection
  //                                  signals like backup failure
  //                                  or LZ-mesh drift) and an ops-
  //                                  bot compromise can't reach
  //                                  the user-alert channels.
  //                                  Healthcheck failures +
  //                                  nightly backup outcomes go
  //                                  to TG_OPS_CHAT_ID.
  // All three of B2_ENDPOINT, B2_BUCKET, and TG_OPS_CHAT_ID are
  // operator-configured via `wrangler secret put` rather than baked
  // into this JSONC file. Reasons:
  //   - B2_ENDPOINT is account-region-specific. The
  //     `scripts/setup-backblaze.mjs` setup script discovers the
  //     account's S3 endpoint from `b2_authorize_account`'s response
  //     and prints it. Hard-coding one operator's region here would
  //     silently break signed B2 calls for any fork / re-deploy on
  //     a different account whose bucket lives in a different
  //     region.
  //   - B2_BUCKET names are globally unique across all B2 accounts.
  //     A fork can't reuse "vaipakam-offchain-data-warm" — they
  //     need their own bucket name.
  //   - TG_OPS_CHAT_ID isn't strictly secret (chat ids grant
  //     nothing without the bot token) but lifting it out of the
  //     public repo avoids revealing which channel ops alerts land
  //     in. Free-of-cost obfuscation.
  // Workers reads `env.X` identically for vars and secrets — no
  // Worker code change is needed.
  // The first monthly / yearly archive THIS deployment wrote — declared,
  // because the healthcheck cannot infer them (#1476). Inferring from the
  // surviving objects is circular: delete the oldest and the inferred
  // baseline advances past it, so the deleted period stops being required.
  //
  // BOTH ARE DELIBERATELY UNSET. The archive→warm rename created a NEW,
  // EMPTY bucket rather than migrating the old one, so this deployment has
  // written no monthly or yearly cut yet. `ARCHIVE_FIRST_MONTHLY="2026-06"`
  // was committed here while it still described the OLD bucket — against
  // the new one it would mark every month from 2026-06 as required and the
  // monthly tier would report FAILED rather than the honest "young bucket,
  // coverage degraded".
  //
  // Set each one once its first cut exists — the 1st of a month for
  // monthly, Jan 1 for yearly — using:
  //     cd ops/offchain-data-warm && npm run archive:baselines
  // While unset, the weekly report says COVERAGE DEGRADED for that tier,
  // which is the truthful state for a bucket with nothing older to lose.
  "vars": {},
}
